    trad_gilgamesh_epoch: 'c. 2100–1200 BCE &nbsp;·&nbsp; Standard Babylonian Version &nbsp;·&nbsp; Cuneiform &nbsp;·&nbsp; Oral-to-written tradition',
    trad_gilgamesh_desc_p1: 'The oldest surviving epic narrative. Gilgamesh, king of Uruk, is two-thirds god and one-third human — larger than life, and unable to come to terms with the human part. After the death of his companion Enkidu, Gilgamesh sets out to find the secret of eternal life. He does not find it. What he finds instead is the oldest recorded argument that a mortal life, fully lived, is enough.',
    trad_gilgamesh_desc_p2: 'The text survives in fragments across hundreds of clay tablets, found in libraries from Nineveh to Megiddo. The most complete version — the Standard Babylonian Version, assembled in the 12th century BCE by the scholar-priest Sîn-lēqi-unninni — is what we have here. Even the Standard Babylonian Version is not complete: dozens of passages survive only as lacunae, damage to the clay that no scholarship has bridged.',
