    parallels_about_heading: 'About structural resonances',
    parallels_about_p1: 'A structural resonance is not a claim that one tradition borrowed from another. It is a documented observation that two or more traditions share a recognisable narrative shape — a similar sequence of events, a similar human situation, a similar structural function for a character or event.',
    parallels_about_p2: 'All parallels on this page have been confirmed by a scholar after review. Computationally-detected candidate parallels are never shown publicly until a scholar has reviewed and confirmed them. The type labels (\"shared human condition\", \"shared narrative shape\", \"shared social pattern\") describe the nature of the resonance — they are not claims about historical contact or origin.',
    parallel_flood_subtitle_refs: 'Gilgamesh (Tablet XI) ↔ Genesis 6–9 (KJV)',
    parallel_flood_body: 'Both traditions depict a divine judgment by flood, a chosen survivor warned through an indirect channel, and birds sent to find dry land. The structure is shared with remarkable precision across traditions separated by culture and century. What the survivor receives diverges entirely: immortality in Gilgamesh, covenant in Genesis.',
    parallel_flood_trad_lines: 'Traditions: Mesopotamian · Hebrew / Biblical',
