    frag_crumb_ep_flood: 'The Flood',
    frag_crumb_ep_sleep: 'Utnapishtim\'s Challenge',
    frag_crumb_ep_plant: 'The Plant of Immortality',
    frag_ep_sleep_title: 'UTNAPISHTIM\'S CHALLENGE',
    frag_ep_plant_title: 'THE PLANT OF IMMORTALITY',
    frag_surf_flood_p1: 'Gilgamesh, stricken by the death of Enkidu and unable to accept his own mortality, seeks out Utnapishtim — the one mortal who has been granted eternal life by the gods. Utnapishtim lives beyond the waters of death, at the ends of the earth. Gilgamesh crosses them.',
    frag_surf_flood_p2: 'At the edge of the world, Utnapishtim tells Gilgamesh a secret: he survived the great flood. The gods had decided to destroy all life. But the god Ea, bound by oath not to warn any human directly, whispered the decision through the walls of a reed house. Utnapishtim heard. He built a boat. He loaded his family, his possessions, and every living creature onto it. The flood came.',
    frag_surf_flood_p3: 'The waters covered the mountains. After seven days the flood subsided. Utnapishtim sent out birds — first a dove, then a swallow, then a raven — to find land. The raven did not return. He disembarked and made sacrifice. The gods, who had not eaten since the flood began, gathered over the offering.',
    frag_surf_flood_p4: 'The goddess Ishtar swore an oath on her lapis lazuli necklace: she would remember these days. The god Enlil, who had decreed the flood, was rebuked by Ea for his disproportionate judgment. As recompense, Enlil granted Utnapishtim and his wife the gift of eternal life and placed them at the mouth of the rivers, at the ends of the earth, where they remain.',
    frag_surf_sleep_p1: 'After the flood tale, Gilgamesh still aches to escape mortal limits. He is not ready to concede.',
    frag_surf_sleep_p2: 'Utnapishtim proposes a stern test of nature: remain awake seven days and seven nights without drifting into sleep — for only strength over sleep bears any analogy to resisting death.',
    frag_surf_sleep_p3: 'Gilgamesh vows to prevail, takes his seat to keep vigil, and falls asleep at once. For each day he lies unconscious, Utnapishtim\'s wife bakes a loaf and sets it beside him; the row of loaves, from fresh to stale, marks the passing time he never felt.',
    frag_surf_sleep_p4: 'When Gilgamesh awakens, he at first denies that he slept; the breads convict him. The meaning is uncompromising: mere flesh cannot conquer sleep — and surely cannot conquer mortality.',
    frag_surf_plant_p1: 'At his wife\'s plea, Utnapishtim relents toward Gilgamesh: there exists a miraculous plant rooted in the abyss beneath the Waters of Death. Its thorns conceal the power of youth renewed.',
    frag_surf_plant_p2: 'Gilgamesh weighs his feet with stones, plunges deep, uproots the plant, and claws his way back to the sunlit shore, holding the ransom against age.',
    frag_surf_plant_p3: 'Before starting the journey home he bathes in a clear pool nearby and lays the harvested plant unattended on the bank — and a snake, drawn by its fragrance, devours what he cannot guard. The serpent slips away, renewing itself by shedding its skin.',
    frag_surf_plant_p4: 'What remains for Gilgamesh is the lesson of the lost gift: renewal passes to the animal that took it; the king returns toward Uruk with empty hands and a clearer measure of his border.',
