    parallel_view_credit_kjv: 'Translation: KJV (1611) · Public domain',
    parallel_view_attr_genesis_excerpt: '— Genesis 6:5–6; 7:5 (KJV)',
    parallel_view_genesis_phase_note: 'Phase 1: Full fragment view for this tradition is not yet available. The Genesis tradition is documented here for comparative purposes; it will be fully onboarded in Phase 2.',
    parallel_view_note_region: 'Scholarly note',
    parallel_view_note_what_head: 'What resonates',
    parallel_view_note_what_p: 'Both narratives depict a deity (or divine council) deciding to destroy humanity with a flood because of human wrongdoing. In both, a single righteous man is warned and instructed to build a boat to preserve life. In both, birds are sent to determine whether the waters have receded. The structural sequence — divine judgment / chosen survivor / vessel / birds / aftermath — is shared with remarkable precision across traditions separated by culture and century.',
    parallel_view_note_why_head: 'Why it resonates',
    parallel_view_note_why_p1: 'The flood functions as a threshold in both traditions: what exists before it and what exists after are fundamentally different. In Gilgamesh, the flood is the mechanism by which a human acquires exceptional standing — Utnapishtim\'s immortality is a direct consequence of his survival. In Genesis, the flood establishes the foundational covenant between the divine and humanity.',
    parallel_view_note_why_p2: 'The shared structural function — flood as world-reordering threshold — is more significant than the surface similarity of boats and birds. Both traditions use the flood not merely as catastrophe but as a pivot on which the relationship between the human and the divine turns.',
    parallel_view_note_diverge_head: 'Where it diverges',
    parallel_view_note_diverge_p1: 'The most significant divergence is in what the survivor receives. Utnapishtim is granted literal immortality — a singular exception the gods declare will not be repeated. This immortality becomes the central rebuke of the Gilgamesh epic: Gilgamesh cannot have what Utnapishtim has. The gate is permanently closed.',
    parallel_view_note_diverge_p2: 'In Genesis, Noah receives a covenant and a promise — the flood will not recur; the rainbow is its sign. Survival leads outward (a renewed world, a new beginning for all) rather than inward (an exceptional individual forever sealed from death). The same structural sequence produces opposite resolutions.',
    parallel_view_note_reveals_head: 'What the divergence reveals',
    parallel_view_note_reveals_p1: 'The Gilgamesh version uses the flood to permanently close the gate on immortality — Utnapishtim\'s survival proves the exception, not the rule, and Gilgamesh\'s encounter with him is a rebuke, not an invitation. The Genesis version uses the flood to open a covenantal relationship between the divine and humanity — it is a beginning, not a terminus.',
    parallel_view_note_reveals_p2: 'Both narratives use the same architecture to answer different theological questions: what separates humans from the divine (Gilgamesh), and what binds the divine to humanity (Genesis). Understanding the shared structure makes the divergence more meaningful, not less. Each tradition\'s answer is sharpened by knowing the other existed.',
    parallel_view_note_scholarship_head: 'Scholarship cited',
    parallel_view_cite_george_html: 'Andrew George, <em>The Babylonian Gilgamesh Epic</em> (OUP, 2003), vol. I, pp. 70–99.',
    parallel_view_cite_speiser_html: 'E.A. Speiser, <em>Genesis</em>, Anchor Bible Commentary (1964), pp. 54–69.',
    parallel_view_cite_tigay_html: 'Jeffrey Tigay, <em>The Evolution of the Gilgamesh Epic</em> (1982), pp. 214–240.',
    parallel_view_cite_heidel_html: 'Alexander Heidel, <em>The Gilgamesh Epic and Old Testament Parallels</em> (1949).',
