    iliad_book16_name: 'Patroclus',
    iliad_book16_desc: 'Achilles allows Patroclus to borrow his armor and lead the Myrmidons. Patroclus drives back the Trojans but defies Achilles\' instructions and presses too far. Apollo strips him of his armor. Euphorbus wounds him first; Hector kills him. Achilles does not yet know.',
    iliad_book16_ep: '4 episodes',
    trad_iliad_epoch: 'c. 8th century BCE (oral tradition c. 12th–9th c. BCE) &nbsp;·&nbsp; Attributed to Homer &nbsp;·&nbsp; Ancient Greek &nbsp;·&nbsp; Dactylic hexameter',
    trad_iliad_desc_p1: 'The Iliad is not a story about the Trojan War. It is a story about a few weeks within the war\'s tenth year, and it is about rage — the rage of Achilles, son of Peleus and the sea-goddess Thetis, and what that rage costs him, his enemies, and the people he loves. The poem begins with a quarrel over a captive woman between Achilles and Agamemnon, the commander of the Greek forces. Achilles withdraws from battle. His withdrawal nearly destroys the Greek cause. His return destroys Hector, prince of Troy, and ends with Achilles giving Hector\'s body back to Priam, his father, in the final act of the poem\'s enormous moral arc.',
    trad_iliad_desc_p2: 'The text survives in approximately 1,800 medieval manuscripts — a transmission record unmatched in antiquity. The standard critical edition follows the work of the Alexandrian scholars of the 3rd–2nd century BCE, particularly Aristarchus of Samothrace, who produced the most influential recensio. The poem was composed in oral tradition before writing, probably first committed to text in the late 8th century BCE; the received text reflects centuries of performance variation before stabilization. 24 books. Approximately 15,693 lines.',
    trad_iliad_cross_tradition_sub: '1 confirmed parallel — the companion\'s death and heroic grief appear in the Mesopotamian tradition',
    iliad_book1_name: 'The Wrath',
    iliad_book1_desc: 'The quarrel between Achilles and Agamemnon. Achilles withdraws from battle. Thetis asks Zeus to honour her son by letting the Greeks suffer.',
    iliad_book1_ep: '4 episodes',
    iliad_book6_name: 'Farewell',
    iliad_book6_desc: 'Hector returns to Troy. His farewell to Andromache and their infant son. The weight of what he knows is coming.',
    iliad_book6_ep: '3 episodes',
    iliad_book9_name: 'Embassy',
    iliad_book9_desc: 'The Greeks send an embassy to Achilles to ask him to return. He refuses. His argument is the most searching rejection of heroic values in the poem.',
    iliad_book9_ep: '2 episodes',
    iliad_book22_name: 'The Death of Hector',
    iliad_book22_desc: 'Achilles pursues Hector around the walls of Troy. They fight. Hector dies. Achilles defiles the body.',
    iliad_book22_ep: '3 episodes',
    iliad_book24_name: 'The Ransom',
    iliad_book24_desc: 'Priam crosses the Greek lines at night to ransom Hector\'s body. Achilles receives him. The two enemies weep together. The poem ends.',
    iliad_book24_ep: '◑ 1 structural parallel · 3 episodes',
    iliad_book24_ep_parallel: '◑ 1 structural parallel',
    iliad_book24_ep_count: '3 episodes',
    iliad_book24_hub_doc_title: 'Book XXIV: The Ransom — Iliad — Mnemosyne Engine',
    iliad_book24_hub_intro: 'The final book of the Iliad. After Achilles kills Hector, he continues to defile the body — dragging it around Patroclus\' tomb each morning. The gods, offended by this treatment, intervene. Zeus sends Iris to Priam and Thetis to Achilles. Priam loads a ransom cart and crosses enemy lines at night, guided by Hermes in disguise. He reaches Achilles and supplicates him directly. Both men weep for their dead. Achilles relents; the body is washed, anointed, and returned. A truce of eleven days is granted for the funeral rites.',
    iliad_book24_rec_pop_detail: 'Book XXIV survives in all major manuscript traditions. However, Aristarchus of Samothrace athetized (bracketed as potentially spurious) approximately 12 lines, including parts of the opening divine assembly. The overall book is secure; specific line attribution remains a matter of scholarly debate.',
    iliad_book24_ep_coming: 'Full fragment view coming in next release',
    iliad_book24_ep1_title: 'THE GODS DECIDE',
    iliad_book24_ep1_desc: 'Zeus calls an assembly after Achilles continues dragging Hector\'s body around Patroclus\' tomb. Iris is sent to Priam with Zeus\'s instructions: go to Achilles, bring ransom, retrieve the body. Thetis is sent to Achilles with a gentle rebuke. The divine machinery that makes the ransom possible is set in motion.',
    iliad_book24_ep1_aria: 'The Gods Decide — episode 1 of Book XXIV — not yet available',
    iliad_book24_ep2_title: 'PRIAM\'S NIGHT JOURNEY',
    iliad_book24_ep2_desc: 'Priam loads a ransom cart with treasures — gold, cauldrons, robes — and sets out alone through enemy lines with his herald Idaeus. Hermes appears disguised as a young Myrmidon soldier, escorts Priam past the Greek sentries, and guides him unseen through the camp to Achilles\' hut.',
    iliad_book24_ep2_aria: 'Priam\'s Night Journey — episode 2 of Book XXIV — not yet available',
    iliad_book24_ep3_desc: 'Priam supplicates Achilles at the knees, kissing the hands that killed Hector. Both men weep simultaneously — Priam for Hector, Achilles for his father Peleus and for Patroclus. Achilles relents. The body is washed, anointed, and wrapped so Priam cannot see the wounds. A shared meal; eleven days of truce; Priam departs before dawn with Hector on the cart.',
    iliad_book24_ep3_aria: 'The Ransom of Hector — has 1 cross-tradition parallel',
